WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:07.560
From modern masterpieces to cult classics, these&nbsp;
frightful films never got a sequel or remake.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.070
"Help!"

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:11.258
"Jay what is it?"

00:00:11.258 --> 00:00:12.579
"Help!"

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.840
The central theme of the 2010 South Korean&nbsp;
horror movie I Saw the Devil is the price of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.240
obsessive revenge. After government operative&nbsp;
Kim Soo-hyun — played by Lee Byung-hun — loses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.520
his pregnant fiancée to Choi Min-sik's Jang&nbsp;
Kyung-chul, he swears vengeance and vows to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:37.080
pick apart the mass murderer's entire&nbsp;
life. Even as Jang adds more victims,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.880
Kim deliberately lets him walk free so&nbsp;
he can continue to stalk and torment him.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:48.240
The Los Angeles Times praised I Saw the Devil&nbsp;
for balancing its bloody mayhem with a realistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.520
emotional undercurrent — its tragedy sets&nbsp;
the movie apart from typical horror films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:59.040
In Paste's retrospective review, they claimed&nbsp;
the film's violent meditation on revenge was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.800
criminally overlooked during its initial release,&nbsp;
and declared it the best horror movie from 2010.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.620
According to The Wrap, the English-language remake&nbsp;
rights to I Saw the Devil were optioned in 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:15.240
with writers Simon Barrett and Adam Wingard&nbsp;
set to tackle the adaptation. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.300
nothing has come of the project yet. In 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.800
Barrett told iHorror.com that&nbsp;
it's probably for the best saying,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.860
"Even though it would have been&nbsp;
much different than the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.328
it would have just probably annoyed some people"

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.540
Long before he helmed The Lord of&nbsp;
the Rings trilogy or King Kong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.980
Peter Jackson crafted low-budget horror&nbsp;
comedies in his native New Zealand. Among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:44.520
the most notorious of Jackson's early work is the&nbsp;
1992 zombie movie, Braindead. Released in North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:51.180
America as Dead Alive, the hilarious cult classic&nbsp;
is considered one of the goriest films ever made.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.000
"I kick ass for the lord!"

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.020
Those bitten by a plague-ridden&nbsp;
rat-monkey from Skull Island at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.920
the Wellington Zoo become feral&nbsp;
and rise as ravenous zombies.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.760
Timothy Balme's Lionel tries to&nbsp;
keep the undead outbreak a secret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.020
even as the number of zombies grows&nbsp;
after his mother Vera becomes infected.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:15.840
With nearly 80 gallons of fake blood&nbsp;
reportedly used for the climactic sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.800
Braindead is Jackson at his most gruesome.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:25.020
While Jackson told Uproxx that he's working&nbsp;
on a 4K re-mastering of Braindead, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.680
about the extent of anything that's&nbsp;
currently happening with the title.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.840
Following an acclaimed career as the&nbsp;
co-creator and co-star of the sketch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.400
comedy series Key &amp; Peele, Jordan Peele&nbsp;
turned his attention to horror. Peele made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.580
his feature debut with 2017's Get Out, acting&nbsp;
as writer, director, and producer for the film.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:49.380
The film follows Chris Washington, played by&nbsp;
Daniel Kaluuya, as he travels with his girlfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:54.360
played by Allison Williams, to meet her family&nbsp;
in upstate New York. Shortly after arriving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.240
Chris discovers that the community harbors a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:01.943
dark secret and that he's the next&nbsp;
target for their insidious goals.

00:03:01.943 --> 00:03:02.693
"Get out!"

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:05.340
"Sorry, man."

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.120
"Okay."

00:03:06.238 --> 00:03:07.138
"Get out!"

00:03:07.138 --> 00:03:07.920
"Yo!"

00:03:08.274 --> 00:03:08.774
"Now."

00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:09.421
"Yo. Chill, man."

00:03:09.421 --> 00:03:10.334
"Get out!"

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:14.940
Peele's story is filled with sharp&nbsp;
satire and biting social commentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:19.380
about the Black experience in the United&nbsp;
States. Named one of the best horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:24.120
films of all time by Rotten Tomatoes, the&nbsp;
movie was a major critical and commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.800
success. The film even won Peele an&nbsp;
Oscar for Best Original Screenplay.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.480
Peele told Business Insider that Get&nbsp;
Out was the first of five socially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.720
conscious horror movies he&nbsp;
planned on making, saying,

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.260
"I've been working on these premises&nbsp;
about these different social demons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:45.960
these innately human monsters that are woven into&nbsp;
the fabric of how we think and how we interact."

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:50.820
Peele's second horror feature, Us, hit&nbsp;
theaters in 2019. His third effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:56.340
Nope, came out in 2022. While these movies&nbsp;
have common social commentaries, to date,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:01.800
they're all considered stand-alone entries.&nbsp;
There are currently no plans to revisit Get Out.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.720
Drew Goddard and Joss Whedon — who had&nbsp;
previously collaborated on Buffy the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:12.000
Vampire Slayer and Angel — reunited for the&nbsp;
critically-acclaimed 2011 feature film, The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.500
Cabin in the Woods. Goddard directed, with Whedon&nbsp;
producing a screenplay that they wrote together.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.760
The Cabin in the Woods sees a group of college&nbsp;
students vacationing at a remote cabin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.080
only to be unwittingly targeted by a&nbsp;
cabal of scientists. The scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.680
must complete a supernatural&nbsp;
ritual to stop the apocalypse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.540
so they unleash a number of common horror&nbsp;
movie monsters to prey upon the students.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:37.380
The Cabin in the Woods leans&nbsp;
into its self-aware storytelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:42.720
poking fun about established horror tropes&nbsp;
while subverting audience expectations. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.880
an interview with Total Film, Whedon referred&nbsp;
to the movie as "a very loving hate letter."

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.300
Although the ending doesn't leave&nbsp;
much to follow up with for a sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.980
that's never stopped a horror franchise&nbsp;
before. However, Goddard told Fandango,

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:02.100
"Every version of continuing the story undercuts&nbsp;
the ending that we had in Cabin, and I just feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:07.200
like that continues to be the perfect ending&nbsp;
for that movie and I never want to undercut it."

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:12.960
Though filmmaker Paul W.S. Anderson may be&nbsp;
best known for helming most of the Resident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:18.960
Evil movies, his finest horror movie is&nbsp;
arguably 1997's Event Horizon. Combining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:24.120
science fiction with supernatural horror, Event&nbsp;
Horizon sees a group of astronauts becoming the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:29.220
victims of a spaceship possessed by demonic&nbsp;
forces. In a behind-the-scenes feature on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:35.118
the Event Horizon DVD, producer Jeremy Bolt&nbsp;
described the film as a ghost story in space.

00:05:35.118 --> 00:05:40.380
"She tore a hole in our universe.&nbsp;
A gateway to another dimension."

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:44.640
Though not financially or critically&nbsp;
successful during its theatrical release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:50.340
Event Horizon found greater success in the home&nbsp;
video market and has since become a bonafide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:55.500
cult classic. In an interview with Inverse about&nbsp;
the move, Anderson remembered a piece of advice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:00.060
actor Kurt Russell gave him in the wake of the&nbsp;
movie's disappointing initial run. Russel said,

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:04.200
"In 20 years, that's going to be the&nbsp;
movie you're really glad you made"

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.800
It seems the actor was right.&nbsp;
While Variety reported in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.100
2019 that a television series&nbsp;
adaptation was in the works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:15.780
there hasn't been any further information&nbsp;
released about a possible follow-up.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:22.020
From Doctor Sleep to Midnight Mass, Mike Flanagan&nbsp;
has become one of the most celebrated horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:27.360
filmmakers to emerge in recent years. One of&nbsp;
Flanagan's earliest features — which he directed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.040
edited, and co-wrote — was the 2013&nbsp;
supernatural horror movie, Oculus.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:35.460
Kaylie and Tim — played by Karen&nbsp;
Gillan and Brenton Thwaites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:39.600
respectively — are siblings investigating&nbsp;
the cursed legacy of a haunted mirror that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:44.280
destroyed their family when they were children.&nbsp;
The story unfolds across two time periods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.240
moving between their childhood trauma&nbsp;
and their present-day investigation.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:53.040
Many of Flanagan's narrative themes seen in his&nbsp;
acclaimed Netflix original series, The Haunting of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:58.740
Hill House, are prominently on display in Oculus&nbsp;
— most notably, the end of childhood innocence.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:03.900
In 2014, Flanagan talked to JoBlo about the&nbsp;
possibility of a sequel. While he noted that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:09.540
he'd be interested in doing one if he found an&nbsp;
exciting story, he had no real interest, saying,

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:13.740
"The last thing I'd want to do is&nbsp;
get in the business of franchising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:17.880
the movie and crank out sequels&nbsp;
to an idea even if it's worn out."

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:24.420
Grief and the loss of family run heavy through&nbsp;
the Australian horror movie The Babadook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:28.500
written and directed by Jennifer Kent&nbsp;
in her feature directorial debut.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:32.880
In the wake of losing her husband, Essie&nbsp;
Davis's Amelia struggles to raise her young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:36.960
son Samuel — played by Noah Wiseman.&nbsp;
This is complicated further when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.860
two are menaced by a supernatural&nbsp;
creature known as the Babadook.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:45.600
Kent strove to utilize practical effects&nbsp;
throughout the entire production. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:50.880
Amelia and Samuel are both poisoned by their&nbsp;
grief, Kent's special effects choices augment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:55.466
the film's theme of how people create&nbsp;
monsters from their personal darkness.

00:07:55.466 --> 00:08:01.586
"You don't know how many times I&nbsp;
wished it was you, not him, that died."

00:08:01.586 --> 00:08:03.840
The Babadook is truly one of a kind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:09.300
and Kent knows this better than anyone. In an&nbsp;
interview with IGN, she claimed that she owns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.320
the film's sequel rights to ensure that&nbsp;
one will never be made. She elaborated,

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:16.800
"It's just not what the film's&nbsp;
about. It would destroy the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:19.740
integrity of everything we&nbsp;
worked so hard to protect."

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:28.020
Sex and death walk hand-in-hand in the 2014 indie&nbsp;
horror flick, It Follows — written and directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:33.420
by David Robert Mitchell. The movie involves a&nbsp;
sexually-transmitted supernatural entity and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:38.700
variety of forms it utilizes to close in on its&nbsp;
prey. Jay Height — played by Maika Monroe — is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:43.860
the latest person to receive this curse and&nbsp;
searches for a way to escape her grim fate.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:49.200
It Follows received considerable critical&nbsp;
acclaim upon its theatrical release in 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.620
with the Rotten Tomatoes critical&nbsp;
consensus describing it as,

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:58.380
"Smart, original, and above all&nbsp;
terrifying, It Follows is the rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:04.080
modern horror film that works on multiple&nbsp;
levels — and leaves a lingering sting."

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:09.660
With its steady escalation of suspense&nbsp;
interwoven with its narrative themes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:16.020
It Follows became a solid success, with Monroe&nbsp;
specifically lauded for her performance. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:21.000
to Entertainment Weekly, though there was some&nbsp;
talk of a follow-up to It Follows in 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:25.020
there has been little information about&nbsp;
any project development since then.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:29.340
Mitchell told Digital Spy that he does have&nbsp;
some ideas of where the story could go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:32.280
but for now he wants to keep it as a standalone.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:38.100
A24 is an independent entertainment company&nbsp;
that has achieved a lot of acclaim since its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:42.360
launch in 2012. For over a decade,&nbsp;
the studio has been releasing many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:47.820
horror film favorites, like Under the&nbsp;
Skin, The Witch, and 2018's Hereditary.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:52.350
Hereditary marked the feature directorial&nbsp;
debut of Ari Aster. Throughout the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:56.940
Aster, who also wrote the screenplay, explores&nbsp;
the dissolution of a family in the face of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:02.760
immense grief. Hereditary begins with a grounded,&nbsp;
relatable tragedy before steadily progressing into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:08.400
outright supernatural terror. After the Graham&nbsp;
family loses their young daughter in a horrific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:14.934
accident, they become targets of a sinister group&nbsp;
involved with demons and malevolent spirits.

00:10:14.934 --> 00:10:20.934
"And look at that pattern. Did you see up there?&nbsp;
This was painted above the body, right? In blood!"

00:10:20.934 --> 00:10:26.700
The film is a searing look at a divided&nbsp;
family, manipulated by forces beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:31.500
their comprehension. It's hard to imagine a&nbsp;
follow-up when Hereditary's stunning finale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:37.500
ends quite conclusively, so perhaps it's for&nbsp;
the best that the story remains untouched.

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:43.320
Channeling the aesthetics and styles of&nbsp;
1980s horror, The House of the Devil is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:48.180
one of the earliest feature films written&nbsp;
and directed by acclaimed filmmaker Ti&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:53.580
West. According to The Independent, West's&nbsp;
quest for authenticity came right down to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:59.400
director shooting the movie on 16mm film&nbsp;
and releasing promotional copies on VHS.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:04.200
College student Samantha Hughes, played&nbsp;
by Jocelin Donahue, answers a call for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:09.180
babysitting gig at a remote mansion, only to&nbsp;
learn that the job is not exactly what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:13.560
advertised. Samantha soon finds herself&nbsp;
in the middle of an occult ritual after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:18.300
discovering the true occupants of the house&nbsp;
were gruesomely murdered by her employers.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:24.360
More than just wearing his influences on his&nbsp;
sleeve, West subverts expectations about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:29.880
genre to keep the audience on its toes, while&nbsp;
building the tension and violence in equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:34.740
measure. A love letter to '80s horror&nbsp;
— right down to its commentary on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:40.500
satanic panic — The House of the Devil features&nbsp;
West's filmmaking strengths on full display.

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:46.500
The New York Times sang its praises for the&nbsp;
film, saying that West's use of setting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:52.800
suspense holds up against works by legendary&nbsp;
filmmakers years his senior. As West has become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.800 --> 00:12:00.060
more widely known for his recent movie trilogy of&nbsp;
X, its prequel Pearl, and the upcoming MaXXXine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:04.200
he seems to have left The House of&nbsp;
the Devil to stand on its own for now.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:12.000
Canadian filmmaker David Cronenberg is widely&nbsp;
recognized as the master of body horror, with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:18.720
1983 film Videodrome being one of the standouts of&nbsp;
his career. Along with Cronenberg's signature gore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:24.660
and grotesque body transformations, Videodrome&nbsp;
tackles mass media consumption — specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:29.460
how the rising UHF television stations&nbsp;
of the time addressed sex and violence.

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:36.060
The film focuses on cable station CEO Max Renn&nbsp;
— played by James Woods — as he begins airing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:41.700
Videodrome, a program from Malaysia that&nbsp;
allegedly consists of actual snuff films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:46.740
After Nicki Brand — played by Debbie Harry —&nbsp;
becomes obsessed with the program and vanishes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:52.140
Max learns that Videodrome actually airs&nbsp;
from Pittsburgh. He decides to investigate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:57.240
further and discovers a surreal and&nbsp;
strange world he never imagined.

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:02.160
"Television is reality, and&nbsp;
reality is less than television."

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:06.900
Videodrome is a surreal exploration&nbsp;
of the infectious impact that media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:10.560
consumption has on the subconscious,&nbsp;
standing out among Cronenberg's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:15.240
most acclaimed films. Paste even&nbsp;
called it his best movie, writing,

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:18.180
"Videodrome is horror down to its bones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:23.640
a shard of phantasmagorical mania wielded&nbsp;
by the genre's most cerebral master."

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:28.920
Back in 2012, Deadline announced that Adam&nbsp;
Berg would direct a remake of Videodrome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:35.340
modernizing the story as a big budget sci-fi&nbsp;
action thriller. As of January 2023, no further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:40.920
information on this remake has been released,&nbsp;
and Cronenberg's classic has remained untouched.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:48.180
If the 1973 classic British film The Wicker&nbsp;
Man originally popularized the idea of folk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:54.000
horror movies, 2019's Midsommar updated the&nbsp;
sub-genre with modern sensibilities and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:58.440
bigger body count. Written and directed&nbsp;
by Ari Aster, the movie takes place in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:03.300
the middle of a summertime celebration in&nbsp;
scenic Sweden. With its sun-soaked setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:08.400
and cheerful residents, Midsommar is a&nbsp;
thoroughly memorable take on the genre.

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:11.820
In the traumatizing aftermath&nbsp;
of losing her entire family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:16.620
Florence Pugh's Dani travels with her boyfriend&nbsp;
and their friends to a midsummer festival in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:22.440
Sweden. The group discovers a strange commune&nbsp;
behind the celebration, with bizarre customs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:28.140
and rituals designed to rob individuals of&nbsp;
their sanity, and eventually their lives.

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:33.240
Aster told Fandango that since they share&nbsp;
some themes, Midsommar can be seen as,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:37.380
quote, "something of a companion to&nbsp;
Hereditary." However, both of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:42.900
two are ultimately stand-alone, and to date,&nbsp;
no followup to Midsommar has been announced.

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:48.840
Robert Eggers' feature film debut, 2015's The&nbsp;
Witch, captured the grim side of early America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:53.700
effectively blending folk and supernatural&nbsp;
horror. Growing up in New Hampshire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:58.680
Eggers was fascinated by the local folklore&nbsp;
fostered by the region's history of witch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:02.520
trials. Discussing his goals for the&nbsp;
film with The Atlantic, Eggers said,

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:05.760
"I wanted to make an archetypal&nbsp;
New England horror story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:10.560
something that would feel like an inherited&nbsp;
nightmare, a Puritan nightmare from the past."

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:15.960
Eggers put his childhood fears into a story&nbsp;
about isolation and being shunned from society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:21.000
causing his protagonists to fall prey to&nbsp;
the horrors existing on the fringe. Set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:25.680
in New England during the 1630s, The Witch&nbsp;
follows a small English family when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:30.000
are banished from their Puritanical colony&nbsp;
for a religious falling out. As they attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:34.200
to run their own homestead, their crops&nbsp;
fail in the face of the impending winter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:39.000
and they are targeted by a local witch&nbsp;
working to tear the entire family apart.

00:15:39.354 --> 00:15:43.473
"You cannot bring the crops to yield.&nbsp;
You cannot hunt. Is that truth enough?"

00:15:43.473 --> 00:15:45.900
"Enough."
"Thou canst do nothing save cut wood!"

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:51.720
Using only natural and period-appropriate&nbsp;
light, The Witch is arguably Eggers at his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:57.660
most atmospheric and unsettling. While outlets&nbsp;
like Rotten Tomatoes named it the best horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:03.060
film of 2016, Eggers told Entertainment Weekly&nbsp;
that he has no plans for a sequel. He stated,

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:06.240
"If I wanted to know what happens&nbsp;
after the last shot of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:08.568
I would have made a longer movie."

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:13.680
One of the most influential visionaries&nbsp;
in horror films is Italian filmmaker Dario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:19.260
Argento. His early work in the giallo subgenre&nbsp;
has inspired countless storytellers across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:24.660
a variety of mediums and earned him great&nbsp;
praise from critics. Garage even called him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:28.980
quote, "the most influential horror&nbsp;
director outside of Hitchcock."

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:34.020
Argento's 1985 film Phenomena follows&nbsp;
Jennifer, played by Jennifer Connelly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:38.520
who travels to a remote all-girls boarding&nbsp;
school in Switzerland. While there, she uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:43.200
her powers to communicate with insects to stop a&nbsp;
serial killer preying on women around the school.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:49.320
Phenomena combined supernatural horror with the&nbsp;
classic giallo trappings of mystery plots with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:55.860
slasher and psychological thriller elements. With&nbsp;
Argento's usual flair for hyper-stylized visuals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.860 --> 00:17:00.420
and a set of insects and arachnids&nbsp;
to match the film's creepy themes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:03.960
Phenomena stands among&nbsp;
Argento's most beloved works.

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:08.820
Though the original international cut of the film&nbsp;
was a heavily edited version titled Creepers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:14.820
Argento fans can see all versions of&nbsp;
the film rendered in 4K Ultra HD in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:18.060
the upcoming 2023 home release by Synapse Films.

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:22.740
While this cult classic went&nbsp;
nearly 40 years without a sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:28.620
that may change soon. In 2022, the Italian&nbsp;
studio Titanus announced that it had plans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:32.760
to make a sequel to Phenomena set in&nbsp;
the modern day with a mix of U.S. and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:38.580
international talents. The film was&nbsp;
in development as of October of 2022.

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:44.400
Adam Wingard's 2014 horror film The Guest&nbsp;
centers around the Peterson family mourning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:48.780
the loss of their son Caleb in the War in&nbsp;
Afghanistan. They're unexpectedly visited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:53.100
by former U.S. soldier David Collins,&nbsp;
played by Dan Stevens, who claims to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:58.320
have been Caleb's best friend overseas. As&nbsp;
the Petersons welcome David into their home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:03.300
increasingly violent acts begin to&nbsp;
occur around the family. David is at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:08.034
the center of this lethal escalation, which&nbsp;
leads them to wonder about his identity.

00:18:08.034 --> 00:18:12.540
"If you brought money for all of them, I'll cut&nbsp;
you a deal. You can take them all off my hands."

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:14.340
"No, I'm going to kill you."

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:20.580
Driven by a darkly charming performance from&nbsp;
Stevens, The Guest doesn't necessarily surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:24.540
its audience with twists, but it still&nbsp;
manages to be a captivating thrill-ride.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:28.860
Beloved by critics upon its release,&nbsp;
it has become a cult hit over time.

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:34.380
In 2021, screenwriter Simon Barrett told&nbsp;
ComicBook.com that a potential follow-up would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:38.640
go in a radically different direction because&nbsp;
he never intended to make a sequel for the film.

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:44.280
Wingard confirmed in 2022 just how different&nbsp;
that direction would be when he released The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:49.140
Guest 2 as a musical soundtrack. In&nbsp;
a press release, Wingard explained,

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:51.960
"Embedded within the song&nbsp;
titles, the album artwork,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:57.720
and the music itself are hidden clues that lead&nbsp;
the listener down the path a sequel would take."

00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:01.620
In an interview with Empire, Wingard&nbsp;
noted that working on the soundtrack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:06.240
inspired him and Barrett, and they're&nbsp;
now considering an actual film or TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:10.440
sequel to The Guest. However, there's no&nbsp;
word yet on if they'll go ahead with it.

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:15.900
One of the most chilling movies to come&nbsp;
out of South Korea is Na Hong-jin's The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:21.360
Wailing. Channeling a mix of horror movie&nbsp;
influences, the 2016 film blends everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:26.640
from body horror and demonic possession to&nbsp;
ghost stories and sociopolitical commentary.

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:32.280
After a mysterious Japanese man — played by Jun&nbsp;
Kunimura — visits a small South Korean town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:37.860
its inhabitants suddenly become enraged and&nbsp;
kill their own families. As two police officers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:43.320
investigate what happened, suspecting the Japanese&nbsp;
man as the culprit, they soon find that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:48.780
paranormal forces at work. The Wailing received&nbsp;
near-universal critical acclaim upon its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:53.160
due largely to its haunting atmosphere&nbsp;
and an unforgettably bleak ending.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:58.320
In 2017, ScreenDaily reported that Ridley Scott&nbsp;
was interested in producing an English-language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:03.360
remake. However, producer Hosung Kim noted&nbsp;
that the setting and sensibility of The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:08.700
Wailing would be difficult to translate into&nbsp;
a Western remake. Though Hosung and Na weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:13.482
completely opposed to the idea, there's&nbsp;
been no word of the reboot since then.

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:19.320
Starting with The Thing in 1982, celebrated&nbsp;
filmmaker John Carpenter launched his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:24.420
Apocalypse Trilogy — a set of standalone&nbsp;
movies that conclude with certain doom for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:30.000
the human race. The second installment of&nbsp;
this informal trilogy is 1987's Prince of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:34.320
Darkness. The film is directed by Carpenter&nbsp;
from a screenplay he wrote under a pseudonym,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:37.478
and it focuses on the arrival of Satan on Earth.

00:20:37.478 --> 00:20:39.973
"Pray for death."

00:20:39.973 --> 00:20:45.360
A quantum physicist and his team are invited to&nbsp;
inspect a strange liquid that they're informed is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:51.240
Satan. The liquid begins to infect and possess&nbsp;
the researchers, with the scientists deducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:55.860
from their recurring dreams that they are&nbsp;
facing an antimatter-fueled apocalypse.

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:57.000
According to Film Cred,

00:20:57.000 --> 00:20:59.580
"The concepts behind Prince of Darkness came from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:03.360
Carpenter’s interest in the utterly&nbsp;
uncanny world of quantum physics."

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:09.300
Prince of Darkness sees Carpenter at his&nbsp;
most surreal, with audiences and critics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:14.580
marking it among his most underrated works.&nbsp;
Carpenter told Consequence of Sound in 2018&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:19.200
that he was considering turning Prince of&nbsp;
Darkness into a TV series with Shudder. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:23.400
further explained to Fandom in 2022 that&nbsp;
he's open to making a sequel to the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:27.180
because of the many fun directions they&nbsp;
could go with its subject matter. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:31.276
no official plans have been&nbsp;
made for a sequel or series.

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:34.560
Spanish filmmaker J.A. Bayona leaned heavily into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:38.460
classic horror influences for&nbsp;
their 2007 film, The Orphanage.

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:42.180
Belén Rueda's Laura returns to&nbsp;
the orphanage where she grew up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:46.380
with the intention of reopening it as a&nbsp;
children's clinic. Her plans get thwarted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:51.240
when her adopted son — played by Roger Princep&nbsp;
— goes missing. As Laura searches for her son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:56.400
she discovers the orphanage harbors a dark&nbsp;
secret and a haunted legacy of its own.

00:21:56.400 --> 00:22:01.740
Screenwriter Sergio G. Sanchez told The Deadbolt&nbsp;
that his main influences in crafting the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:06.600
were literary works: J.M. Barrie's Peter&nbsp;
Pan inspired him to imagine the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:10.920
a runaway child like Wendy Darling from a&nbsp;
parent's perspective, while Henry James' The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:15.780
Turn of the Screw gave him the framework for a&nbsp;
child-centered ghost story full of adult themes.

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:20.220
The Orphanage was a critical&nbsp;
and box office hit in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:24.360
becoming the highest-grossing movie in&nbsp;
Spain. Not surprisingly, an American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:29.700
remake was almost immediately announced in that&nbsp;
same year. However, there has been little news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:34.800
surrounding the proposed project since 2011,&nbsp;
when it was reported on by Bloody Disgusting.

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:42.600
The final horror movie that Dario Argento directed&nbsp;
in the '80s, 1987's Opera, stands among his best.

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:47.580
Released on American home video as Terror at&nbsp;
the Opera, the film is centered on a young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:52.200
opera singer — played by Cristina Marsillach&nbsp;
— finally getting her big break to headline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:57.120
an Italian opera. Her dreams are crushed&nbsp;
when a horrific killing spree breaks out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.120 --> 00:23:00.960
in the opera house, with the murders&nbsp;
resembling the traumatizing death of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:05.700
her mother years prior. She then sets out&nbsp;
to discover the masked killer's identity.

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:10.920
After numerous projects exploring the&nbsp;
supernatural, Argento returned to his more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:16.500
grounded giallo roots for Opera. A gruesome&nbsp;
murder mystery, Opera is one of Argento's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:22.800
most critically-acclaimed films, with Slant&nbsp;
calling it "his last full-fledged masterpiece."

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:28.740
Slick and stylish, Opera demonstrates&nbsp;
how far Argento had grown as a filmmaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:33.000
setting a high-water mark for the giallo&nbsp;
subgenre that has seemingly yet to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:39.240
surpassed. This may also be part of the reason&nbsp;
why no one has tried to remake or revisit it.

